      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-349828",
      ":question/problem": "The temperature outside is measured in degrees Celsius $(.^{\u2218}C)$. On the Celsius scale, $0^{\u2218}C$ means the temperature at which water freezes.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n| A temperature of $-3^{\u2218}C$ means that it is $3$ degrees below zero. | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | ---: |\n| If the temperature rises from $-3^{\u2218}C$ to $2^{\u2218}C$, it increases by $1$ degree. | {{field-2}} |\n| A temperature of $0^{\u2218}C$ means there is \"no temperature.\" | {{field-3}} |"
